<h1>1. INTRODUCTION</h1>

<h2>1.1 Background</h2>
<p>
The credibility of modern democratic elections depends on the integrity, transparency, and non-repudiation of the voting apparatus. Traditional manual voting processes—utilizing paper ballots, physical voter registries, and indelible ink—are prone to human errors, booth capturing, ballot stuffing, voter impersonation, and costly counting logistics.
</p>
<p>
Relational Database Management Systems (RDBMS) like Oracle Database 21c offer enterprise features including ACID transaction compliance, strict domain constraints, cryptographic indexing, row-level concurrency control, and Pluggable Database (PDB) multi-tenancy. Applying an enterprise RDBMS to election engineering ensures that voter registration, biometric template lookup, and tallying are mathematically consistent, resilient to power failures, and transparently auditable.
</p>

<h2>1.2 Overview of the Proposed System</h2>
<p>
<strong>SecureVote</strong> is a full-stack, database-backed electoral voting system. It combines biometric identity verification with an anonymous voting pipeline. The system enforces a strict operational separation between three primary stakeholders:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>System Administrators:</strong> Configure candidates, supervise workstation endpoints, open/close polling windows, and generate real-time tabular tally summaries.</li>
  <li><strong>Booth Operators / Staff:</strong> Authenticate against Oracle using salted PBKDF2 credentials, search voter rolls by legal name or ID number, verify citizen biometrics, and authorize voter sessions.</li>
  <li><strong>Voters (Citizens):</strong> Enrolled citizens who authenticate their identity via fingerprint minutiae matching (or simulated bypass for testing), receive a single-use authorization token, and cast an untraceable ballot for their chosen candidate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>1.3 Organization of the Report</h2>
<p>
Chapter 2 presents the problem statement, objectives, and comparisons with existing systems. Chapter 3 defines project scope and system requirements. Chapter 4 outlines system analysis, use cases, and Data Flow Diagrams. Chapter 5 covers database design, entity relationships, data dictionary, and BCNF normalization proofs. Chapter 6 details table creation, queries, and implementation architecture. Chapter 7 documents test strategies and verification results. Chapter 8 presents conclusions and future work.
</p>

<h1>2. PROBLEM STATEMENT AND OBJECTIVES</h1>

<h2>2.1 Problem Statement</h2>
<p>
Conventional electoral systems suffer from two structural vulnerabilities:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Voter Impersonation & Multiple Voting:</strong> Paper electoral rolls cannot dynamically verify whether an individual has already voted in another voting booth across town on polling day.</li>
  <li><strong>Breach of Ballot Secrecy in Electronic Systems:</strong> Digital voting databases often improperly link voter identities with cast ballots by recording foreign keys or fine-grained timestamps in vote tables, enabling side-channel correlation attacks.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.2 Objectives</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Centralized Relational Integrity:</strong> Implement an Oracle 21c XE database schema that maintains eligible citizens, candidates, and cast votes with strict relational constraints.</li>
  <li><strong>Zero-Trace Ballot Anonymity:</strong> Guarantee voter privacy by completely decoupling voter identity from cast ballots, strictly forbidding foreign keys, identifiers, or fine-grained timestamps in the <code>VOTE</code> table.</li>
  <li><strong>1:1 Biometric Authentication:</strong> Verify citizens against encrypted biometric minutiae templates stored in the database (<code>VOTER.fingerprint_template</code>), locking records after cast submission via atomic transactions.</li>
  <li><strong>Automated Fraud Prevention & Lockouts:</strong> Restrict failed verification attempts using an automated security lockout mechanism (<code>VERIFICATION_LOCKOUT</code>) and prevent replay attacks using single-use tokens (<code>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Auditable Logging:</strong> Record all booth check-ins, verification approvals, denials, and operator actions into an immutable audit table (<code>VERIFICATION_LOG</code>).</li>
</ol>

<h2>2.3 Existing System and Limitations</h2>
<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Metric</th>
    <th>Existing Paper / Traditional EVM System</th>
    <th>Proposed SecureVote Oracle System</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Identity Validation</strong></td>
    <td>Manual photo comparison; prone to fake IDs</td>
    <td>Automated biometric 1:1 matching against Oracle BLOB</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Duplicate Voting</strong></td>
    <td>Reliant on physical indelible ink markings</td>
    <td>Instant Oracle check on <code>has_voted = 'Y'</code> constraint</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Ballot Privacy</strong></td>
    <td>High in paper box; low in un-normalized SQL databases</td>
    <td>Cryptographically decoupled relational schema</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Result Tallying</strong></td>
    <td>Days of manual counting; high labor cost</td>
    <td>Instantaneous SQL aggregation via analytical queries</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><strong>Audit Trail</strong></td>
    <td>Paper logs that can be lost or stolen</td>
    <td>Immutable, structured <code>VERIFICATION_LOG</code> in Oracle</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>2.4 Proposed System</h2>
<p>
SecureVote addresses existing limitations through a modern database and software architecture. By utilizing an Oracle Pluggable Database (<code>XEPDB1</code>) with dedicated schema privileges (<code>SECUREVOTE_APP</code>), citizen eligibility is checked dynamically. When an enrolled citizen checks in, the system verifies <code>VOTER.has_voted = 'N'</code>. Upon verification, the server generates a 120-second JWT authorization token. When the vote is submitted, this token is permanently logged into <code>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</code> and <code>has_voted</code> is updated to <code>'Y'</code> in an atomic transaction.
</p>

<h1>3. SCOPE AND REQUIREMENTS</h1>

<h2>3.1 Project Scope</h2>
<p>
<strong>In Scope:</strong> Citizen voter registration with national ID hashing and biometric template encryption; operator authentication and role-based permissions; searchable voter check-in; biometric 1:1 template matching with test bypass mode; anonymous ballot casting; real-time tally aggregation; security lockout tracking.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Out of Scope:</strong> Direct integration with national civil registration servers over wide-area internet; postal or remote internet-based absentee voting.
</p>

<h2>3.2 Functional Requirements</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>FR-1 (Citizen Enrollment):</strong> The system shall register eligible citizens by capturing full name, government ID type, ID number, and fingerprint image bytes.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-2 (Deduplication Check):</strong> The system shall compute a salted SHA-256 hash of the ID number and verify uniqueness against <code>VOTER.id_hash</code> before enrollment.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-3 (Voter Lookup):</strong> The system shall support rapid search across eligible voters using case-insensitive name matching.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-4 (Verification & Token Issuance):</strong> The system shall verify citizen biometrics, verify that <code>has_voted = 'N'</code>, ensure polling is open, and issue a single-use JWT authorization token.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-5 (Ballot Submission):</strong> The system shall accept a candidate selection with a valid authorization token, record an anonymous entry in <code>VOTE</code>, update <code>VOTER.has_voted = 'Y'</code>, and invalidate the token in a single atomic transaction.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-6 (Audit Logging):</strong> Every verification attempt (Allowed, Denied, Duplicate, Lockout) shall be logged in <code>VERIFICATION_LOG</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>FR-7 (Election Lifecycle Control):</strong> Administrators shall have the exclusive privilege to toggle election states (<code>open</code> vs <code>closed</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>3.3 Non-Functional Requirements</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Security:</strong> Passwords hashed with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (100,000 rounds). Biometric minutiae encrypted with application-level AES-256-GCM before BLOB insertion.</li>
  <li><strong>Data Integrity:</strong> Enforced at schema level through Primary Keys, Foreign Keys, Unique Keys, and <code>CHECK</code> constraints (<code>has_voted IN ('Y', 'N')</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Performance:</strong> Sub-20ms query response time on voter lookup using B-Tree indexes on <code>VOTER(name)</code> and <code>VOTER(has_voted)</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Reliability & ACID Compliance:</strong> Database transactions use atomic commit and automatic rollback semantics on exceptions.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3.4 Hardware and Software Requirements</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hardware:</strong> Intel Core i5/i7 or AMD Ryzen processor; 8 GB RAM minimum; 20 GB available SSD storage; USB fingerprint scanner (or software bypass test module).</li>
  <li><strong>Software:</strong> Windows 10/11 or Ubuntu Linux 22.04 LTS; <strong>Oracle Database 21c Express Edition (XE)</strong> (Pluggable Database: <code>XEPDB1</code>); Python 3.11+ with <code>python-oracledb</code> driver; FastAPI framework; Node.js 20+ with Next.js 16 and Tailwind CSS.</li>
</ul>

<h1>4. SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND DESIGN</h1>

<h2>4.1 System Architecture</h2>
<p>
The system follows a three-tier architecture:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Presentation Tier:</strong> Next.js 16 (React 19) client providing the Booth Kiosk (<code>/booth</code>), Admin Console (<code>/admin</code>), and Registration Portal (<code>/enroll</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Application Tier:</strong> FastAPI backend handling REST routing, JWT security token generation, biometric template evaluation, and database connection pooling.</li>
  <li><strong>Data Tier:</strong> Oracle Database 21c XE managing data storage across 8 relational tables with transactional guarantees.</li>
</ol>

<h2>4.2 Use-Case Descriptions</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Citizen Voter:</strong> Presents legal identification, undergoes biometric verification, receives single-use authorization token, views secret electronic ballot, submits untraceable vote.</li>
  <li><strong>Booth Operator:</strong> Authenticates via username/password, searches registered voter list by name or ID, initiates biometric check, monitors check-in authorization.</li>
  <li><strong>System Administrator:</strong> Enrolls candidates, opens/closes election polling, reviews audit verification logs, views live election turnout summaries.</li>
</ul>

<h2>4.3 Data Flow Architecture</h2>
<p>
<strong>Context Level (Level-0):</strong> Voters provide legal credentials and biometric samples to SecureVote. The system validates information against Oracle Database 21c and returns ballot access or denial notifications. Staff operators receive system logs and election tallies.<br>
<strong>Level-1 Process Breakdown:</strong> 
(1.0) Search and identify voter record &rarr; 
(2.0) Decrypt and match biometric template &rarr; 
(3.0) Mint single-use JWT authorization token &rarr; 
(4.0) Record anonymous ballot, update citizen status to voted, and consume token in an atomic transaction &rarr; 
(5.0) Generate real-time turnout metrics for administrators.
</p>

<h2>4.4 Process Description and Business Workflows</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Citizen Check-in:</strong> Operator searches for an enrolled citizen. The system executes a case-insensitive query against <code>VOTER</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Eligibility Pre-check:</strong> If <code>has_voted == 'Y'</code>, access is rejected immediately (<code>DUPLICATE_ATTEMPT</code>). If <code>failed_count >= 3</code>, access is blocked (<code>LOCKED_OUT</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Biometric Verification:</strong> The server reads encrypted template BLOB from <code>VOTER</code>, decrypts it in-memory via AES-256-GCM, and compares it to the incoming scan (or bypass simulation).</li>
  <li><strong>Token Generation:</strong> A cryptographically signed token is issued containing the citizen identifier and a random UUID token ID (<code>jti</code>) valid for 120 seconds.</li>
  <li><strong>Ballot Submission:</strong> The citizen selects a candidate. In a single atomic transaction:
    <ul>
      <li>Verify <code>token_jti</code> is not in <code>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</code>.</li>
      <li>Insert <code>token_jti</code> into <code>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</code>.</li>
      <li>Update <code>VOTER</code> set <code>has_voted = 'Y'</code>, <code>voted_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>.</li>
      <li>Insert into <code>VOTE</code> a random <code>vote_id</code>, <code>candidate_id</code>, and <code>vote_date = TRUNC(CURRENT_DATE)</code>.</li>
      <li><code>COMMIT</code> transaction.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ol>

<h1>5. DATABASE DESIGN</h1>

<h2>5.1 Entity–Relationship (ER) Model</h2>
<p>
The database architecture enforces an unbreachable separation of concerns:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>ADMIN_USER</code> maintains system operator credentials independently.</li>
  <li><code>ELECTION_STATE</code> controls election lifecycle status.</li>
  <li><code>CANDIDATE</code> maintains political candidates, related to <code>VOTE</code> via a 1-to-many relationship.</li>
  <li><code>VOTER</code> stores citizen eligibility and encrypted biometrics.</li>
  <li><strong>Critical Privacy Constraint:</strong> There is strictly <strong>NO relationship</strong> (no foreign keys or voter identifiers) between <code>VOTER</code> and <code>VOTE</code>.</li>
  <li><code>VERIFICATION_LOG</code> records all authentication attempts for auditability.</li>
  <li><code>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</code> tracks single-use JWT tokens to prevent replay attacks.</li>
  <li><code>VERIFICATION_LOCKOUT</code> monitors failed attempts per citizen to enforce temporary security lockouts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>5.2 Relational Schema</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>ADMIN_USER</strong> (<u>admin_id</u>, username, password_hash, role, created_at) &mdash; <em>PK: admin_id, UK: username</em></li>
  <li><strong>ELECTION_STATE</strong> (<u>state_key</u>, state_value, updated_at) &mdash; <em>PK: state_key</em></li>
  <li><strong>CANDIDATE</strong> (<u>candidate_id</u>, name, party, description, created_at) &mdash; <em>PK: candidate_id</em></li>
  <li><strong>VOTE</strong> (<u>vote_id</u>, candidate_id*, vote_date) &mdash; <em>PK: vote_id, FK: candidate_id &rarr; CANDIDATE(candidate_id)</em></li>
  <li><strong>VOTER</strong> (<u>voter_id</u>, name, id_type, id_last4, id_hash, fingerprint_template, has_voted, enrolled_at, voted_at) &mdash; <em>PK: voter_id, UK: id_hash</em></li>
  <li><strong>VERIFICATION_LOG</strong> (<u>log_id</u>, voter_id, attempted_at, result, reason, workstation_id) &mdash; <em>PK: log_id</em></li>
  <li><strong>CONSUMED_VOTE_TOKENS</strong> (<u>token_jti</u>, consumed_at) &mdash; <em>PK: token_jti</em></li>
  <li><strong>VERIFICATION_LOCKOUT</strong> (<u>voter_id</u>, failed_count, locked_until, last_failed_at) &mdash; <em>PK: voter_id</em></li>
</ul>

<h2>5.3 Data Dictionary</h2>

<h3>Table: ADMIN_USER</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column Name</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Null?</th><th>Constraints</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>admin_id</td><td>VARCHAR2(36)</td><td>No</td><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>UUID identifier for staff member</td></tr>
  <tr><td>username</td><td>VARCHAR2(50)</td><td>No</td><td>UNIQUE</td><td>Operator login handle</td></tr>
  <tr><td>password_hash</td><td>VARCHAR2(255)</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 salted password hash</td></tr>
  <tr><td>role</td><td>VARCHAR2(20)</td><td>No</td><td>CHECK(role IN ('admin','booth-operator','operator'))</td><td>Operational access tier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>created_at</td><td>TIMESTAMP</td><td>No</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td><td>Staff onboarding timestamp</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Table: VOTER</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column Name</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Null?</th><th>Constraints</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>voter_id</td><td>VARCHAR2(36)</td><td>No</td><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>Unique UUID assigned during enrollment</td></tr>
  <tr><td>name</td><td>VARCHAR2(100)</td><td>No</td><td>Indexed</td><td>Legal citizen name</td></tr>
  <tr><td>id_type</td><td>VARCHAR2(50)</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>Government document classification</td></tr>
  <tr><td>id_last4</td><td>CHAR(4)</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>Last 4 digits for masked operator lookup</td></tr>
  <tr><td>id_hash</td><td>VARCHAR2(64)</td><td>No</td><td>UNIQUE</td><td>Salted SHA-256 hash of national ID</td></tr>
  <tr><td>fingerprint_template</td><td>BLOB</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>AES-256-GCM encrypted minutiae bytes</td></tr>
  <tr><td>has_voted</td><td>CHAR(1)</td><td>No</td><td>CHECK(has_voted IN ('Y','N'))</td><td>Real-time ballot issuance flag (Default: 'N')</td></tr>
  <tr><td>enrolled_at</td><td>TIMESTAMP</td><td>No</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td><td>Registration timestamp</td></tr>
  <tr><td>voted_at</td><td>TIMESTAMP</td><td>Yes</td><td>None</td><td>Timestamp when ballot was authorized</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Table: CANDIDATE</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column Name</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Null?</th><th>Constraints</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>candidate_id</td><td>VARCHAR2(36)</td><td>No</td><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>Unique candidate identifier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>name</td><td>VARCHAR2(100)</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>Full name of running candidate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>party</td><td>VARCHAR2(100)</td><td>No</td><td>None</td><td>Sponsoring political party or independent</td></tr>
  <tr><td>description</td><td>VARCHAR2(500)</td><td>Yes</td><td>None</td><td>Platform campaign summary</td></tr>
  <tr><td>created_at</td><td>TIMESTAMP</td><td>No</td><td>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</td><td>Nomination recording timestamp</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Table: VOTE</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Column Name</th><th>Data Type</th><th>Null?</th><th>Constraints</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td>vote_id</td><td>VARCHAR2(36)</td><td>No</td><td>PRIMARY KEY</td><td>Random UUID identifying cast ballot</td></tr>
  <tr><td>candidate_id</td><td>VARCHAR2(36)</td><td>No</td><td>FOREIGN KEY REFERENCES CANDIDATE</td><td>Candidate receiving ballot</td></tr>
  <tr><td>vote_date</td><td>DATE</td><td>No</td><td>Indexed</td><td>Calendar date only (excludes time to prevent correlation)</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.4 Database Normalization</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>First Normal Form (1NF):</strong> All attributes contain atomic, indivisible values. Repeating groups and multi-valued fields are eliminated. Every table possesses an unambiguous primary key.</li>
  <li><strong>Second Normal Form (2NF):</strong> All relations satisfy 1NF. Because all primary keys are single-attribute identifiers (UUIDs), partial dependencies do not exist. Every non-key attribute is fully functionally dependent on the entire primary key.</li>
  <li><strong>Third Normal Form (3NF):</strong> All relations satisfy 2NF. Transitive functional dependencies are absent. In <code>VOTER</code>, the functional dependencies are:
    <ul>
      <li><code>voter_id &rarr; {name, id_type, id_last4, id_hash, fingerprint_template, has_voted, enrolled_at}</code></li>
      <li><code>id_hash &rarr; voter_id</code> (Candidate Key)</li>
    </ul>
    No non-key attribute determines any other non-key attribute.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF):</strong> For every non-trivial functional dependency <code>X &rarr; Y</code>, the determinant <code>X</code> is a superkey. In <code>ADMIN_USER</code>, both <code>admin_id</code> and <code>username</code> serve as candidate keys, satisfying BCNF.</li>
</ul>

<h2>5.5 Integrity Constraints</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Primary Key Constraints:</strong> Enforce unique entity identity on <code>admin_id</code>, <code>voter_id</code>, <code>candidate_id</code>, <code>vote_id</code>, <code>state_key</code>, <code>log_id</code>, and <code>token_jti</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Referential Integrity:</strong> <code>VOTE.candidate_id</code> references <code>CANDIDATE(candidate_id)</code>. Orphan ballot entries are prevented.</li>
  <li><strong>Domain Constraints:</strong> <code>VOTER.has_voted IN ('Y', 'N')</code> and <code>ADMIN_USER.role IN ('admin', 'booth-operator', 'operator')</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Unique Identity Constraints:</strong> <code>VOTER.id_hash</code> is declared <code>UNIQUE</code>, mathematically preventing duplicate voter registrations with the same national ID.</li>
</ul>

